  "answer": "The study confirmed 4 of the 5 cases it was asked for, each on a different scene. They are 0d4893f5 at 1.2x / \u22120.5 s, 0e002edd at 1.4x / 0 s, 18f8dbd6 at 1.4x / 0 s and 1bbe02fc at 1.2x / \u22120.5 s. The fifth candidate, 18d28973 at 1.4x / \u22121 s, failed only about half the time, and its range does not clear 0.5. None of the triaged failures involved the pedestrian. The ego drifted left into a car in the next lane, cut a right turn into parked cars, failed to brake for a slowing car ahead, or was flagged off-road. Every failure happened at 1.2x or 1.4x speed, and on the scenes tried at several step-out times, changing the pedestrian's timing did not change the outcome. So these cases show what makes the policy fail at higher hand-off speed, not what makes it fail with the pedestrian. Two of the four cases are weaker than the rest. The 1bbe02fc failures are marginal off-road flags that may come from the map's road edge. The 0e002edd failures are all marked as possible artifacts, but the triage reads them as real over-tight turns by the policy, not rendering faults.",
